  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Fri May 22 2026\n\nAn upper-level trough over the southwestern US will move into the \nsouthern Plains over the weekend, with a cold front tracking \nsouthward into northwest Texas by Saturday morning. Ahead of the \nfront, a warm, moist airmass will remain in place across the\nregion with dewpoints in the 60s. This will make for a decent \nenvironment for thunderstorm development, which much of the area \nwill potentially see on Saturday afternoon and evening along and \nahead of the cold front. Severe chances look confined to our far \nsoutheast counties because this is where the best upper-level \nsupport will be. Regardless, heavy rain and flooding concerns look\nto continue for our eastern counties where PWAT values over 1.5 \ninches will be prevalent. \n\nThe upper-level trough is expected to drift further eastward into\neastern Texas by Sunday where it will become a bit more defined \nbefore becoming a closed low on Monday. This progression would \nplace west central Texas on the backside of the low which is less \nfavorable for precipitation overall. Given the persistently humid \nairmass, we'll keep low chances for rain in our far eastern \ncounties for Sunday. The Memorial Day holiday, likewise, looks \ndrier, with low rain chances and highs in the 80s. Some global \nmodels are showing a stronger upper-level trough digging southward\nfrom the Pacific Northwest through much of the Western US by mid \nnext week, with a series of shortwave disturbances tracking\nthrough West Central Texas through early next weekend. This will\nbring another round of potentially active weather through west \ncentral Texas, with high temperatures near to slightly below \nnormal for next week.",
